# Kafka events

Generated .NET 8 library using Confluent.Kafka 2.12.0 and JsonSchema.Net 7.3.4.
Static plaintext JSON Kafka operations only. Provision topics separately.

```csharp
using __NAMESPACE__;
using __NAMESPACE__.Events;
using var runtime = KafkaDefaults.Connect();
// await SendOrderEvent.SendAsync(runtime, payload, headers, key, cancellationToken);
// using var subscription = ReceiveOrderEvent.Subscribe(runtime);
// var record = subscription.Poll(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5), cancellationToken);
// Process(record.Payload); record.Ack();
```

Messages are shared canonical model declarations; operation classes live in `Events/`.
Schemas validate before send and after receive. Unsupported schema keywords and bindings
are rejected during generation. Headers are closed string objects; keys are optional UTF-8
strings. Missing and explicit-null model fields retain the shared model presence semantics.

Send returns native broker delivery metadata. Native idempotent producer retries are enabled;
this does not deduplicate separate application calls or provide transactions/exactly-once.
Cancellation stops waiting and does not retract queued messages. Producers are cached per
operation client ID. Dispose runtime to flush producers, and dispose subscriptions separately.

Consumers start new groups at earliest, auto-commit and auto-offset-store disabled. `Ack()`
commits the delivered record only after processing. Acknowledge before the next poll; handler
failure leaves pending records uncommitted. Close/reopen for replay. Malformed messages poison
the subscription without committing; close/reopen before retrying. Poll, Ack and Dispose must
run on one thread. CancellationToken cancellation is cooperative with <=250 ms poll intervals.
A conflicting fixed group binding override is rejected.

No TLS/SASL, schema registry, transactions, tombstones, automatic handler retry or other
brokers are supported in this output. Runtime validation errors are JsonException; broker
errors retain Confluent.Kafka exception types.

Generate HTTP/GraphQL clients in separate output packages; combining their independently
owned project files and model namespaces in one Kafka package is rejected.
